// ======== Layer 3, owned credit: a buffer's death gives its credit back ========
//
// A sink that is handed a chunk may pass it on to a driver instead of copying it. The chunk is then
// moved, and the credit must come back when the driver is done with it, which only its death says.
// 'chunkHome(src)' makes an IoHome (iobuf) whose lender is the source: the sink calls
// 'buf.setHome(&home)' on each chunk before it moves it away, and when that buffer dies, on whatever
// thread (the I/O thread when a send completes, a worker when a core drops it), the source gets
// 'giveBack' of a fresh empty buffer on the same block, so its window counts the capacity back. The
// source's ChunkSource trait and its actors are unchanged.
//
// RULES. A homed buffer that the sink also gives back by hand ('src.giveBack(b)', 'link.give(b)') would
// return its credit twice: the sink calls 'b.clearHome()' first (a chunk it keeps staged or hands back
// is a plain buffer). The home holds a handle to the source, so the source lives while any of its
// buffers are out. After the runtime stops (actors.isRunning() false) a death sends nothing: the block
// goes to the pool and the credit is moot. The check narrows the race with the stop; it does not close it.

// The lender: the source handle is the home's ctx, moved into its record and dropped with it
fn chunkHomeGive(src: &ChunkSource, b: IoBuf) {
  if !actors.isRunning() {return;}
  src.giveBack(b);
}

fn chunkHomeRelease(src: &ChunkSource) {}

pub fn chunkHome(src: ChunkSource) IoHome {
  IoHome.make[ChunkSource](src, &chunkHomeGive, &chunkHomeRelease)
}
